A use-after-free of the chaining block after an in-place code-buffer flush.

Since upstream ebf7a5d294 (QEMU 10.2), a vCPU in serial context -- every
machine with one vCPU, MTTCG or not, since CF_PARALLEL needs max_cpus > 1 --
empties a full code buffer inside tb_gen_code() and goes on translating
instead of leaving to the main loop.  cpu_exec_loop() then chains the block
that ran before (last_tb) to the new one.  last_tb was in the buffer just
emptied: tb_add_jump() patched a jump into whatever code now lies there and
put the stale pointer on the new block's incoming-jump list, and the next
invalidation of that block (tb_jmp_unlink) followed it into host code and
crashed.  A Win98 DOS box running Duke Nukem 3D, which patches its own
renderer and so fills the buffer, died this way within minutes
(tools/win98-game-test.sh, 2026-10-07).

The loop now notes the flush count around tb_gen_code() and drops last_tb
when it moved.

diff --git a/accel/tcg/cpu-exec.c b/accel/tcg/cpu-exec.c
index a677261..e7bfae6 100644
--- a/accel/tcg/cpu-exec.c
+++ b/accel/tcg/cpu-exec.c
@@ -1024,11 +1024,24 @@ cpu_exec_loop(CPUState *cpu, SyncClocks *sc)
             if (tb == NULL) {
                 CPUJumpCache *jc;
                 uint32_t h;
+                unsigned flushes = qatomic_read(&tb_ctx.tb_flush_count);
 
                 mmap_lock();
                 tb = tb_gen_code(cpu, s);
                 mmap_unlock();
 
+                /*
+                 * With one vCPU, tb_gen_code empties a full code buffer in
+                 * place and carries on (upstream ebf7a5d294), so last_tb
+                 * may now be memory a new block's code or struct occupies:
+                 * chaining it would write a jump into that code and put
+                 * the stale pointer on the new block's jump list, where
+                 * the next invalidation of it walks into garbage.
+                 */
+                if (unlikely(qatomic_read(&tb_ctx.tb_flush_count) != flushes)) {
+                    last_tb = NULL;
+                }
+
                 /*
                  * We add the TB in the virtual pc hash table
                  * for the fast lookup
